The `/session` command currently prints only:

```
session: 'self-improvement-8' (id=...) turn=N max_turns=1000
```

To emit the `[turn N | tool calls: ... | tokens: X in / Y out | max_turns: ...]` line there, the `/session` handler needs access to the *last* turn's token usage and tool-call count. That data lives in the session's `events.jsonl` log as `turn_complete` (`tool_calls`) and `llm_response` (`usage`) entries. The `Session` struct already exposes `events()` and `total_turns()`.

Here is the patch I'd apply to `crates/hanihi-cli/src/main.rs` — replacing the `/session` branch and adding a helper that reads the last completed turn's stats from the log:

```diff
                     "/session" => {
+                        let (tok_in, tok_out, tool_calls) =
+                            last_turn_stats(session);
                         println!(
-                            "session: '{}' (id={}) turn={} max_turns={}",
+                            "session: '{}' (id={}) turn={} max_turns={}\n[turn {} | tool calls: {} | tokens: {} in / {} out | max_turns: {}]",
                             session.name,
                             session.id,
                             session.turn,
-                            agent.max_turns()
+                            agent.max_turns(),
+                            session.turn,
+                            tool_calls,
+                            tok_in,
+                            tok_out,
+                            agent.max_turns(),
                         );
                         continue;
                     }
```

And the helper (placed after `run_turn` or near the other free functions):

```rust
/// Token usage and tool-call count for the most recent completed turn,
/// read from the session event log. Returns (in, out, tool_calls), all 0
/// when no turn has completed yet.
fn last_turn_stats(session: &hanihi_core::session::Session) -> (u64, u64, usize) {
    let mut tin = 0u64;
    let mut tout = 0u64;
    let mut tool_calls = 0usize;
    let mut last_turn = None;

    let Ok(entries) = session.events() else {
        return (0, 0, 0);
    };

    // Walk once, keeping only data belonging to the last completed turn.
    for entry in &entries {
        use hanihi_core::session::log::LogEntry;
        match entry {
            LogEntry::TurnComplete { turn, data, .. } => {
                last_turn = Some(*turn);
                tool_calls = data.tool_calls;
                // reset per-turn counters when a new turn boundary starts
                tin = 0;
                tout = 0;
            }
            LogEntry::LlmResponse { turn, data, .. } if last_turn == Some(*turn) => {
                tin += data.usage.input_tokens as u64;
                tout += data.usage.output_tokens as u64;
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    (tin, tout, tool_calls)
}
```

Note: I renamed the helper to `last_turn_stats` to match your short function-name rule. The log is walked once: `turn_complete` marks the start of the last turn, and subsequent `llm_response` entries within that turn accumulate input/output tokens. If no turn has completed, everything is 0 and the line still prints.

Want me to apply this patch and run `cargo check`/tests?

